course: dictionary
lesson: 8597
title: Scorn and how it shows
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Scorn is a strong feeling that someone or something is worthless or stupid. It is stronger than just not liking something. When a character feels scorn, the author often shows it through what they do, say, or how they look.
explain: «The bully looked at the new boy with scorn and laughed.» The look and the laugh tell you the bully thinks the new boy is worthless. You do not need the word feeling; the clues show it.
explain: Scorn can hide behind polite words. «Bless your heart» in the American South can sound sweet, but it can mean «you poor foolish thing», so the sweetness hides scorn or pity. Read the whole sentence and ask what the speaker really thinks.
fact: Scorn means a strong feeling that someone or something is worthless or stupid.
fact: To scorn someone means to show you think they are worthless.
fact: To scorn something means to refuse it proudly.
fact: Useful phrases: «pour scorn on», «with scorn», and «an object of scorn».
fact: Scorn is also a noun, so you can write «She felt scorn», and a verb, so you can write «She scorned his help».
exercise: «The villain looked at the hero with scorn.» What does with scorn show? | He is very frightened of the hero | He wants to be the hero's best friend | *He thinks the hero is worthless | He is feeling sleepy
exercise: «The old king spoke contemptuously to his servants.» Find and copy the word that means with scorn. | king | *contemptuously | servants | spoke
exercise: Ella says scorn is just another word for dislike. Why is that not quite right? | Dislike and scorn mean exactly the same, so Ella is right | *Scorn is a strong feeling that someone is worthless, which is stronger than dislike | Scorn means being happy about someone | Dislike is a small feeling, but scorn means thinking someone is worthless
test:«She scorned the old map and walked the other way.» What does scorned mean here? | Read it carefully | Copied it out | Lost it in the rain | *Refused it proudly
test:«Bless your heart» can hide what behind sweet words? | A sneeze | Only happiness | Nothing at all | *Pity or scorn
exam: Which sentence uses scorn as a verb? | They poured scorn on the idea. | *The coach scorned the cheat's excuse and sent him off. | The scorn in the room was loud. | The crowd felt scorn for the cheat.
